Talk by Eric Raymond

Early 1990s, during Unix Wars
autoconf did the necessary job of discovery, but it made overly verbose Makefiles that were hard to read

gpsd was refactored in C99 and SUS interfaces with no problems building.  So checks for lack of these were not needed anymore.

Now tools like “autodafe” (sp?) can “deconfig” and “makemake” and create a patch to remove unnecessary checks.  Using a patch format promotes change transparency.

Sometimes nonportable features are needed, though.  Need something like an autotools config.h.  Use -D options to gcc.

With a clean Makefile, then need to test for things like non-POSIX FSF libs. He made a drop-in-replacement for “configure” tool in 650 lines of Python.  What tests to do?  Selected by “magic comments”in Makefile.

Q: Why not do something like CMake?
A: No additional dependencies
“I hate those things!  They all suck!”

Q: What if shell script would suffice?  Why use Make with a bunch of phony rules?
A: Makefile are easier to read and maintain.  People know what to expect and how to interpret.

Q: Why not use kernel config system menuconfig?
A: Too large and too specialized.  Small and simple is better.

Q: libtool?
A: It’s black box hacks to build shared libraries portably.

Q: Trend toward static compilation and no shared libs?
A: I love it.  Much simpler.  Shared libs were for expensive memory and disk space.  These are cheap now.

Q: Assumption of infinite memory in a transpiler?
A: Off topic.

Q: Google “C isn’t a programming language anymore.”  C is a protocol.
A: I’ve read it.
